WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:04.870
Safe and honest: preservatives, labels, NAFDAC and batch records.

2
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.770
This is the video for module 3 of Organic Skincare and Hair Products.

3
00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:22.920
Bacteria, yeasts and moulds need water to multiply. In a lotion, they can grow into very large numbers within days in warm weather, often without anything you can see or smell.

4
00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:31.680
In Nigeria, cosmetics are regulated by NAFDAC. Every word on your label and your WhatsApp posts is a promise.

5
00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:37.880
And batch records let you prove your quality, and find the cause when something goes wrong.

6
00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:51.870
An antioxidant, like vitamin E or rosemary extract, slows oils going rancid. It does not stop germs. Essential oils and grapefruit seed extract are not preservatives either.

7
00:00:53.120 --> 00:01:00.390
A broad-spectrum preservative works against bacteria, yeasts and moulds. Any product containing water needs one.

8
00:01:00.740 --> 00:01:06.670
Watch for hidden water: aloe vera, rose water, herbal teas and milk all bring water in.

9
00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:18.890
Grace in Benin City sees a video of a natural whipped lotion: shea butter, aloe vera gel and vitamin E. She marks the hidden water. Aloe gel is mostly water.

10
00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:26.390
She looks for a broad-spectrum preservative. There is none. Vitamin E is only an antioxidant.

11
00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:32.160
Unpreserved, it would last only days, and customers keep jars for months.

12
00:01:33.410 --> 00:01:41.640
She decides not to sell it. She makes a water-free whipped shea instead, and tells customers to apply it to damp skin for a lighter feel.

13
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:53.330
Lotions for sale need a tested preservative, the correct pH and laboratory testing. Until then, it is honest to say you make only water-free products.

14
00:01:54.580 --> 00:02:11.640
Once you claim to treat, cure or prevent a condition, you are making a medical claim that needs approval as a medicine. Never say cures, heals, guaranteed growth or lightens. Chemical-free is never true, and do not say certified organic without certification.

15
00:02:12.890 --> 00:02:22.810
Say what you can prove. Joy in Owerri changed her post to: light scalp oil, testers with dry scalps found it light, not greasy. Patch test first.

16
00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:29.480
And never add hydroquinone, mercury, steroid creams or crushed tablets. They cause serious harm.

17
00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:41.420
Your label should show the product name, how to use it, the full ingredient list, the net content, and your name and address. Add the batch number, date made and best-before date.

18
00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:51.660
Include warnings like patch test before use, contains nut oil, and keep out of reach of children. Check the current NAFDAC labelling rules before you print.

19
00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:59.270
Registration starts with your business name at CAC, and your brand name with the Trademarks Registry.

20
00:03:00.520 --> 00:03:05.620
Prepare your premises and keep good records from day one, because an inspection follows.

21
00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:22.000
You apply online for each product, with label artwork and a laboratory certificate of analysis. Fees apply, and the guideline states 120 working days. Check current details on the NAFDAC website, because they change.

22
00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:34.270
Give every batch a number, and write it on every jar. Ifeoma's customer said her hair oil smelled like old groundnut after two months. Ifeoma asked for the batch number.

23
00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:39.070
The batch record showed the olive oil came from a new supplier.

24
00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:45.970
Her retained sample from that batch also smelled stale. Samples from other batches were fine.

25
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:52.850
She replaced the bottle, messaged the other buyers of that batch, and stopped using that supplier.

26
00:03:54.100 --> 00:04:02.230
Your best-before date can never be longer than your shortest-lived ingredient. Start modest, and check retained samples every month.

27
00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:14.790
To remember. Anything with water needs a real preservative, and vitamin E is not one. Make only honest cosmetic claims. Put full, true information on your label.

28
00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:19.810
And keep a batch number, a batch record and a retained sample for every run.

29
00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:33.600
Your turn. Audit three online recipes for hidden water and a real preservative. Draft a full label and three honest claims. Make your batch record template, and keep a labelled retained sample.

30
00:04:33.950 --> 00:04:35.890
Continue with the lessons below.
